WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910
- [Narrator] Your dog is the best.

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:03.850
You go on long walks together,

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.782
cuddle up on the couch,
and enjoy sloppy kisses.

00:00:07.782 --> 00:00:09.930
You really love that pup,

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.180
but what's going on there,

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.103
does your dog love you back?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.940
First, let's address the
bigger philosophical question.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.180
Does your dog love anything?

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.490
Can your dog love?

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:29.490
- I think that in many ways we
could apply the same criteria

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.777
to dogs and other
non-human animals as we do

00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:34.726
to human animals.

00:00:34.726 --> 00:00:36.660
- [Narrator] That's Marc Bekoff.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.034
He spent a lifetime
understanding animal minds

00:00:39.034 --> 00:00:44.034
from coyotes and wolves to
domestic dogs like yours.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:49.110
And he says dogs probably think
and feel similar to humans.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.931
- [Marc] A lot of new research
on neuroimaging using FMRI's

00:00:52.931 --> 00:00:57.490
is showing that the
same parts of dog brains

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.670
and human brains light up, for example,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.560
when they're feeling jealous.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.160
- [Narrator] So, really,
the difference between

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.820
how your dog feels joy and other emotions

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.370
and how you experience them
might be like the difference

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.890
between you and another human.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.139
So, dogs can likely
experience what we'd think of

00:01:15.139 --> 00:01:17.710
as loving relationships,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.483
and studies show that dogs are pretty good

00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:22.140
at building relationships with us too,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.820
comes as no surprise.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.042
One study found that dog
brains are highly adapted

00:01:26.042 --> 00:01:31.042
to recognize human faces and
interpret our social cues.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.276
So, maybe that's why your
dog is always extra sweet

00:01:33.276 --> 00:01:35.980
when it knows you've have a bad day.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.504
But are those puppy dog eyes just for you,

00:01:37.504 --> 00:01:42.504
or is your dog pretending to
love you for, you know, food.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.520
- [Marc] And that I think
is really ridiculous.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.740
Are there dogs that use
people for certain things?

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.600
Well, I'm sure there are,
just like there are people

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.926
who use people for certain things,

00:01:50.926 --> 00:01:53.357
but I don't think that
that would characterize

00:01:53.357 --> 00:01:57.840
the overall nature of
dog-human relationships.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.470
- [Narrator] So, yes,
your dog can love you.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.473
Oh, all right, great, we can all home.

00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:05.740
Welp, no, just because they can,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.830
doesn't automatically mean that they do.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.085
- Each dog is a unique individual

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:12.565
and it's not one size fits all.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.970
Some of the most brutally
abused dogs I've met,

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.418
who took months to even get
them to make eye contact

00:02:19.418 --> 00:02:23.811
with me and/or other humans,
have the capacity to love,

00:02:23.811 --> 00:02:28.406
but you had to earn it, it
wasn't just sort of here it is.

00:02:28.406 --> 00:02:30.830
No, you had to earn their trust.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.830
- [Narrator] And like
everything worth having in life,

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.559
that trust will take time and work,

00:02:35.559 --> 00:02:37.780
but it can be done.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.250
- [Marc] You sometimes just go,
good dog, when they've quote

00:02:42.314 --> 00:02:44.560
done nothing, and I say, "Good
dog" to dogs all the time

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.430
and people go, well, why did you do that,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.380
they didn't do anything.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.837
And I would always just
say, "because they're alive

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.327
"and they're good dogs."

00:02:53.513 --> 00:02:54.346
I don't know.

00:02:54.346 --> 00:02:56.817
It's like human beings
saying to a good friend,

00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:00.280
"I love you" or "I'm really
glad to be your friend."

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.240
- [Narrator] And don't worry,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.811
you'll know when you've
earned your pooch's trust.

00:03:03.811 --> 00:03:06.335
- [Marc] Do they follow you around?

00:03:06.335 --> 00:03:08.780
Do they just hang out with you,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.120
not only when you're feeding
them or walking them?

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.170
Do they follow you around,
do they seek you out?

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.770
Do people tell you, if you
will, when you're not there

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.993
that they wander around having wanderlust?

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.010
Is it clear that they miss you?

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.500
- [Narrator] Oh, yeah, checks
out, that's a happy dog.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.240
So, can your dog love you?

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.750
Absolutely.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:32.900
Does your dog love you?

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.733
Well, that's on you.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.300
If you want to learn more

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.872
about how your dog experiences the world,

00:03:41.872 --> 00:03:45.619
check out Marc Bekoff's recent
book, Canine Confidential:

00:03:45.619 --> 00:03:48.243
Why Dogs Do What They Do.

